madam president the majority as consent  the quorum call be suspended without  objection madam president has been a  great source of debate over the past  couple years over a very basic question  how was jeffrey epstein able to take his  own life in federal prison on august 10  2019  last week we found some answers in the  new york times  it pointed out a series of stunning  failures within the bureau prisons  according to the times i quote the newly  obtained records offer no support to the  explosion of conspiracy theories that mr  epstein's death was not a suicide  but they do paint a picture of  incompetence and sloppiness  by within by someone within the bureau  prisons  that incompetence and sloppiness was  evident from the moment jeffrey epstein  entered the metropolitan correctional  center  on his intake sweeping screening form mr  epstein was described as quote a black  male close quote with no prior record of  sex offense convictions  a five-second  google search  would tell you that not only was jeffrey  epstein white  he was one of the most notorious sex  traffickers in recent history and what  about the night he died he'd already  attempted suicide in the weeks leading  up to his death so you would think that  the officials in the bureau prisons  would keep a close watch over this  potentially suicidal inmate in fact the  opposite happened  jeffrey epstein was left alone  unmonitored in a cell  the two officers on duty were allegedly  dozing off and scrolling on their phones  and the next morning epstein was found  dead with a bed sheet tied around his  neck  because of these failures the survivors  of epstein's despicable crimes we'll  never get to see him brought to justice  six months after epstein died former  attorney general bill barr  hand-picked michael carvajal to lead the  bureau of prisons  it could have been a new beginning for  the bureau a chance to get to the bottom  of widely publicized neglect and  mismanagement and to hold the  appropriate parties accountable but that  fresh start never came because since day  one director carvajal of bureau of  prisons has shown no intention of  reforming that institution  consider the case of the warden who ran  the metropolitan correctional center the  night jeffrey epstein died  how did director carvajal respond to  that warden's failure of leadership  well if you can imagine it he actually  rewarded him  director carvajal appointed the same  warden to lead a different facility the  largest federal prison in the united  states  fci fort dixon new jersey and it seems  that warden hasn't learned any lessons  in leadership since mr epstein's death  just last month an inmate in fci fort  dix was attacked from behind and stabbed  in the eye  these are not isolated incidents for  years the bureau of prisons has been  plagued by corruption  chronic understaffing and misconduct by  high-ranking officials in the nearly two  years since director carvajal took  control of the bureau he has failed to  address the mounting crises in our  nation's federal prison system it is far  past time for new reform-minded  leadership in the bureau of prisons  a few weeks ago the associated press  published a breathtaking investigation  into the bureau they concluded that it  is quote a hotbed of abuse  graft and corruption  and has turned a blind eye to employees  accused of misconduct  since 2019 madam president more than 100  federal prison workers have been  arrested for  charged with or convicted of crimes  including sexual abuse murder  and introducing contraband into prison  altogether these crimes account for  two-thirds of the criminal cases against  all department of justice personnel even  though bop employees comprise less than  a third of the workforce i want to share  a few of the stories from the associated  press report  at one federal prison in mississippi an  official responsible for investigating  the misconduct of other staff members  was arrested for his own misconduct he  was not only accused of stalking and  harassing his fellow employees  but he was allowed to remain in his  position  he was even authorized to continue  investigating one of his accusers  in california a warden of a federal  prison women's prison was arrested and  indicted earlier this year for molesting  an inmate  they even kept lewd photos of the victim  on his government-issued cell phone  he allegedly told the woman that she  shouldn't even try to report the assault  because he was quote close friends with  the official who would investigate it  the list goes on it's a recurring  pattern of misconduct by officials  within the bureau of prisons who believe  that they can abuse inmates and break  the law with impunities in some cases  that's exactly what they've done  the details in the ap investigation are  shocking  and for those of us who've been  following director carmel hall's tenure  they are the latest disturbing examples  of failed leadership  time and again he has categorically  failed to uphold his chief  responsibility as director and what is  that responsibility protecting the  health and safety of inmates and  correctional officers today the bureau  suffers from chronic and significant  understaffing director carvajal has  failed to contain outbreaks of covet 19  which has led to tens of thousands of  inmates and staff contracting the virus  and when it comes to reforming our  federal prison system he's failed  in 2018 congressional democrats and  republicans came together on an  overwhelming bipartisan basis to enact  the first step act it was an historic  piece of reform legislation to create  new pathways for prison inmates to  better themselves while they serve their  time so they could return to society  once released nearly three years later  the bureau president still hasn't  implemented most of these reforms one  example under the first step act  low-risk inmates are eligible to earn  time credits reducing their sentences  they do this by completing programs  designed to prevent them from committing  another crime  last month the inspector general at the  department found that the bureau of  prisons has not applied these earned  time credits to any of the approximately  60 000 eligible inmates  now the senate judiciary committee which  i chair is charged with overseeing the  bureau of prisons we've tried to get  answers from dr from director carvajal  and his team  we've repeatedly requested information  we've asked for details about the inmate  banking system now there's a heck of a  story  purportedly has little oversight by the  bureau of prison has allowed inmates to  avoid paying child support restitution  to crime victims  other obligations the bureau reportedly  allowed larry nasser  the so-called doctor who abused hundreds  of young gymnasts before he was  sentenced to life in prison to spend  thousands of dollars from his bureau  presence account on himself  but pay only three hundred dollars  toward the debt he owes his victims  the bureau has delayed responding to our  questions or just flat out ignored him  it's past time to replace director  carvajal this can't wait any longer the  associated press's revelation shows us  that lives are literally at stake i know  attorney general garland understands the  urgency i'm calling on him today to move  immediately to dismiss director carvajal  and to bring real reform to direct to  the bureau of prisons madam president i  yield the floor  you
 

